Did you read my letter below? I keep thinking about our plans.

I finally got round to framing the map you gave us. It is hanging in the hall=
 now, and visitors always stop to look for the places they know.

Mum is settling into the new flat. She likes being closer to the shops, and t=
he lady across the hall has already invited her to the weekly coffee morning.

We drove up to the lakes for a day last week. The leaves were just starting t=
o change, and we had a picnic by the water before the clouds came in.

The school play was a great success. The children had clearly practised for w=
eeks, and the costumes were remarkable considering most of them were made fro=
m old curtains.

The children are back at school and the house is very quiet. I have been catc=
hing up on the jobs I kept putting off over the summer, starting with the cup=
board under the stairs.

Our holiday photos are finally sorted into albums. It brought back lovely mem=
ories, and we laughed at the one of you trying to put up the tent in the wind.

I bumped into your cousin at the station last week. She looked well and asked=
 to be remembered to you; she is still working at the library and seems to en=
joy it.

Our walk on Sunday took us past the old mill. They are turning it into flats,=
 but the water wheel is being kept, which seems a nice way to remember what i=
t was.

We went to the farmers' market on Saturday and came home with far too much ch=
eese. The bread stall was excellent too, and the man there remembered us from=
 last time.

My sister is expecting her second child in the spring. We are all delighted, =
and her eldest has already announced that he would like a brother, please.

Thinking of you.

--=20
Jamie

